Compare and contrast the legal precedents set by Lochner v. New York and Cooley v. Board of Wardens in relation to labor laws.
In Lochner v. New York (1905), the US Supreme Court held that a New York law limiting bakery employee hours to under 10 hours a day and 60 hours a week violated the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, citing a "right of freedom of contract." This decision effectively protected the right of employers and employees to negotiate their own working conditions.

In contrast, Cooley v. Board of Wardens (1852) held that a Pennsylvania law requiring ships to hire local pilots did not violate the Commerce Clause of the Constitution, as the state had the power to regulate pilots. This decision established that states have some authority to regulate commerce, as long as it does not conflict with federal law.

The key difference between the two cases is that Lochner v. New York protected the right of employers and employees to negotiate their own working conditions, while Cooley v. Board of Wardens allowed states to regulate commerce, but only to a limited extent. In other words, Lochner v. New York prioritized individual rights and freedom of contract, whereas Cooley v. Board of Wardens prioritized state regulation of commerce.
