Determine the geographical location of Sarcobatus Flat in relation to the Amargosa River and the surrounding landscape, including its elevation, proximity to other notable features, and any relevant geographical information.
Based on the provided context, Sarcobatus Flat is a closed valley in western Nye County, Nevada, located between Goldfield and Beatty. It is situated in the Central Nevada Desert Basins and is part of the Tonopah Basin. The flat is bounded by the Bullfrog Hills to the south, the Grapevine Mountains and Bonnie Claire Flat to the west, Pahute Mesa to the east and north, and is contiguous with Lida Valley and Stonewall Flat to the north.

In relation to the Amargosa River, Sarcobatus Flat is separated from the Amargosa Valley of the Mojave Desert to the south. The lowest area within the flat is a salt flat just east of Bonnie Claire, with an elevation of 3943 feet. Grapevine Peak, which rises to an elevation of 8737 feet, is located along the crest of the Grapevines adjacent to the California border, approximately six miles west of the Sarcobatus floor in the southern portion of the flat.

